#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d63e99a-9db4-565e-97c5-ffc3090b0074" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e1f14f-36a3-5fb5-af37-568e066845ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286967} {:answer/id #uuid "de6eac51-5ed0-5ae3-ad45-4c8e808a1e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286968} {:answer/id #uuid "faf62a37-808a-5111-a65a-fbc2c71853dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286969} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa26fc9-f7c0-536a-85e7-fb53721fb180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286970} {:answer/id #uuid "7d956354-a411-5f73-9663-586be922e240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e1f14f-36a3-5fb5-af37-568e066845ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286967} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d7d72e0-4d11-52ca-80cb-919ecfd57e61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba2d9110-9750-574a-89bb-6445eeb6564f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73432" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{4}+9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+9.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 9 & 25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{({x}^{4}+9)}\\,{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
